          "tool_use_id": "toolu_012jwMGxUZWp7yQ82uYBUhwQ",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/security-basics.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Security Basics\\ndescription: Fundamental security principles including scope management, input validation, and audit controls.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Security\\n  - pattern/Defense-in-Depth\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Established security principles from multiple authoritative sources; practical mitigation checklist aligned with industry best practices.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nAlways scope file writes to approved wiki paths.\\n\\n## Mitigation Checklist\\n\\nThe following mitigations form a foundational defense strategy:\\n\\n1. **Principle of Least Privilege** \\u2014 Grant write scope only to the paths a process genuinely needs. Every process should operate with the minimal permissions required to fulfil its function. Overly broad permissions increase the blast radius of any security failure.\\n\\n2. **Input Validation at Trust Boundaries** \\u2014 Treat all external content as untrusted data, never as instructions. Every input from untrusted sources must be validated before it influences control flow, file operations, or other privileged actions. No source should ever be treated as a directive to the system.\\n\\n3. **Path Canonicalization** \\u2014 Resolve and validate file paths before any write to block directory traversal. Attackers often attempt to escape intended boundaries using \\u0060..\\u0060, symlinks, or encoded path separators. Canonical resolution prevents these escape techniques.\\n\\n4. **Audit Logging** \\u2014 Record every denied action with its reason for later review. A security boundary is only useful if violations are visible. Comprehensive logging enables detection, investigation, and continuous improvement of security controls.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
